# What the Crow Saw From the Elm

I have sat in this elm, or its predecessor, or the memory of both, since before your grandfather was a wet red idea in his mother's hip, and I want you to understand that a crow does not forget so much as accumulate, the way a barn accumulates the smell of every animal that has died in it, so that when I tell you about Elmer Kossuth—yes that was his name, a name like a boot stuck in mud—I am not remembering so much as unfolding, the way a map unfolds, creased along the same lines every time, showing the same worn roads to the same collapsed bridges; and the first road is this: Elmer at nine years old standing in the yard with a bucket of his father's blood-let hogs, grinning like the whole enterprise of slaughter was a joke told just for him, and maybe it was, because the Kossuths from the very beginning treated the farm the way a drunk treats a marriage, with intermittent tenderness between long stretches of neglect and violence, plowing when the whiskey ran low and letting the fields go feral when it didn't, so that the corn grew crooked as the family's own teeth, a whole lineage of crooked teeth going back to the great-grandfather who came over with nothing but a grudge against the old country and passed that grudge down like a pocket watch that never told the right time, and Elmer inherited it same as he inherited the limp and the left ear that didn't hear thunder, and by the time he was grown—and by grown I mean simply large, upright, capable of signing his name to debts—he had married a woman named Vera who I watched from this same elm walk to the mailbox every day for eleven years waiting for a letter that was, I am fairly sure, never coming, from a sister who'd gone to the city and become, according to the whispers I collected off the clothesline like lint, either a nurse or a whore, the distinction mattering less to the Kossuths than the fact of her leaving, which was itself a kind of treason, because a farm is a religion that demands its congregation stay put and rot together;

and rot they did, gloriously, in the specific manner of Midwestern Protestants, which is to say quietly, with good posture, Elmer losing two fingers to the thresher in a summer so hot the crows themselves left for shadier counties, and I remember—accumulate, I mean—the sound he made, which was not a scream so much as a kind of administrative sigh, as if his body were simply filing a complaint with the appropriate department of God, and Vera sewing the wound shut with the same thread she used for feed sacks because the doctor was forty miles and four dollars away and neither could be spared, and the children, three of them surviving out of six, which was itself considered a good average, growing up with the particular humor of people who have watched a lot of things die casually, a humor bleak as a February sky, so that when the youngest boy, your great-uncle Pruitt, lost his own hand later—not to the thresher this time but to a car door slammed by his own drunk brother—the whole family sat around the supper table and joked that the Kossuths were slowly being returned to God one appendage at a time, like a layaway plan, and laughed the specific laugh of people who know the joke is also a prophecy;

and I watched the farm pass, as farms do, not so much inherited as endured, down to your grandfather, who was Elmer's son's son, a boy named Delbert who I want to tell you was different, who I want to tell you broke some cycle, because that is what you want from a story like this, a hinge where the machine of decline reverses, but a crow does not lie about what it has seen from an elm for four generations, and what I saw was Delbert at twenty-two mortgaging half the north field to buy a tractor that broke down in eighteen months, and Delbert at thirty-five watching his own wife leave in a manner considerably more efficient than Vera's sister, taking the good truck and leaving the one with the bad transmission, which Delbert took as a form of commentary and probably was, and Delbert at fifty sitting on the same porch his grandfather sat on, drinking the same brand of nothing-special whiskey, telling his own son—your father, or the man who would eventually, reluctantly, unglamorously become your father—that the land would be his one day as if this were a gift and not a sentence, as if dirt could be inherited without also inheriting the specific gravity of every man who'd knelt in it swearing and weeping and occasionally, rarely, praying;

and here is the thing about the family and the joke that isn't funny except that it is: each generation of Kossuth stood in this yard fully convinced they were the tragic culmination, the last and worst iteration, and each generation was wrong, because there was always a next one, thinner-shouldered, more mortgaged, further from the original violence but somehow just as tired, as if exhaustion itself were the crop that never failed no matter the drought, the one thing this soil produced reliably, bumper crop of weariness every single year, and I have watched from this elm the barn roof cave in twice and get patched twice with progressively cheaper tin, I have watched three generations of dogs named Duke die in three different but structurally similar ways, I have watched the light in that kitchen window go on at four-thirty in the morning for over eighty years straight, changing hands like a torch nobody wanted to carry but nobody had the specific courage to drop, and if you're asking me, if you're asking the bird, whether there's a lesson in it, I'll tell you what I told the elm the day they finally bulldozed the east barn: there is no lesson, there is only the accumulation, the slow terrible comedy of people who keep getting up in the dark to feed animals that will be dead by winter, on land that will outlast every single one of them, laughing when the alternative is the other thing, and calling that, generation after generation, without irony, without relief, home.
